  "Question": "Solve the equation \\((x-1)^2=9\\). (Separate the two roots with a comma.)",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "4，-2",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": true,
  "Solution": "Taking square roots of both sides gives \\(x-1=3\\) or \\(x-1=-3\\), so \\(x=4\\) or \\(x=-2\\).\n\nAlternative method 1: Factor a difference of squares\n\nThe equation equates two squares, so subtract \\(9=3^2\\) and factor their difference:\n\\[\n(x-1)^2-3^2=(x-4)(x+2)=0.\n\\]\nA product is zero if and only if at least one factor is zero. Thus \\(x-4=0\\) or \\(x+2=0\\), giving the two roots \\(4,-2\\).",
